'''Physics education research''' ('''PER''') is a form of [[discipline-based education research]] specifically related to the study of the [[Physics education|teaching and learning of physics]], often with the aim of improving the effectiveness of student learning. PER draws from other disciplines, such as [[sociology]], [[cognitive science]], [[education]] and [[linguistics]],<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Docktor |first1=Jennifer L. |last2=Mestre |first2=José P. |date=2014-09-16 |title=Synthesis of discipline-based education research in physics |journal=Physical Review Special Topics - Physics Education Research |volume=10 |issue=2 |article-number=020119 |doi=10.1103/PhysRevSTPER.10.020119|doi-access=free |bibcode=2014PRPER..10b0119D }}</ref> and complements them by reflecting the disciplinary knowledge and practices of physics.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Council |first=National Research |url=https://nap.nationalacademies.org/catalog/13362/discipline-based-education-research-understanding-and-improving-learning-in-undergraduate |title=Discipline-Based Education Research: Understanding and Improving Learning in Undergraduate Science and Engineering |date=2012-05-21 |isbn=978-0-309-25411-3 |language=en |doi=10.17226/13362}}</ref> Approximately eighty-five institutions in the United States conduct research in science and physics education.

{{Short description|Proposed field studying the welfare of living beings}}
{{Use British English|date=April 2026}}
{{Use dmy dates|date=April 2026}}
[[File:Yew-Kwang Ng.webp|thumb|[[Yew-Kwang Ng]], who proposed welfare biology in 1995]]

'''Welfare biology''' is a proposed [[interdisciplinary]] field studying the welfare of living beings in relation to their environment. The economist [[Yew-Kwang Ng]] proposed it in 1995, defining welfare as enjoyment minus suffering and combining [[welfare economics]] with [[evolutionary biology]] to investigate the effects of natural processes on sentient individuals.

The proposal has been discussed in [[animal ethics]], [[animal welfare science]], [[ecology]] and research on [[wild animal suffering]]. It includes the study of wild animals, whose individual welfare has received comparatively little attention in conventional animal welfare research. Proposed subjects of investigation include population dynamics, life history, habitat conditions and the effects of environmental change. Researchers disagree on whether welfare biology should be a separate discipline or an extension of existing animal welfare science.

{{Short description|Broadening awareness and understanding of physics}}
